ies" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115415 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115415 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115415 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115415 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ab69be82-a737-5ca5-bd57-46d2a37c64bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116243 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116243 #edb/ref 1061 3201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116243 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cdc53761-47a4-5dc1-a8a0-b21a01b5b35a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116243 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116244 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116244 #edb/ref 1061 3202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116244 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eef16282-4281-509e-8f6c-106a707fcb20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116244 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116245 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116245 #edb/ref 1061 3205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116245 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a370cd83-ae02-58f0-b487-2c4e2f90db1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116245 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116246 #edb/ref 1061 3208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116246 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "634432ad-9cd7-5544-b7bb-1b5664235ef4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116246 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116417 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the values of $x$ that satisfy *either* $4x+2 < 6$ *or* $2x \\geq 8.$\n\nTo do this, we solve each inequality individually and then compute the union of the two solutions.\n\nWe start by solving $4x+2 < 6\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2 & <6 \\\\ 4x & <4 \\\\ x & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/0d/0d36b8e68d9fd0b33b255502860481eeb525b60382029b17434bfb072cc39da2.png)\n\nThen, we solve $2x \\geq 8\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & \\ge 8 \\\\ x & \\ge 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ed/edf5665b7cab7f51e3f252601a96d43c6f0cbfc64e5c9b8da81f0ef09d7b2f5a.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/5c/5c60839a626f2ba8a0fe4a24f4011af0fa52c00d79fbf80039bcfdbdf0a612c7.png)\n\nThus, the final solution is $x < 1$ or $x \\geq 4.$ We can write this using interval notation as\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 1) \\cup [4,\\infty).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116417 #edb/ref 1065 1428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116417 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6d186c13-c1df-52d0-8ed6-a383185138a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116417 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122762 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122762 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122762 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122762 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122762 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122762 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122762 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122762 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "384047e4-5971-5a12-896e-9cbbe096b46a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122763 #edb/ref 1080 "(0, 0)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122763 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122763 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "952b9614-1c48-5e7e-aa39-73bf3282732b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122764 #edb/ref 1080 "(3, 2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122764 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122764 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d19843fd-85d3-577d-9bac-0e1c6682a024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122765 #edb/ref 1080 "(-0.5,-1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122765 #edb/r