 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152310 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152310 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152310 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152310 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152310 #edb/ref 1045 "Understanding Proportional Relationships Using Graphs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152310 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186153196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152310 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152310 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152310 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a9e96cf9-9aad-5b83-81b1-b9488d698494" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152982 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152982 #edb/ref 1061 16271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152982 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3e1f0c98-c1f6-59bc-8387-51338e8475ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152982 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152983 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152983 #edb/ref 1061 16272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152983 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "65b0ae62-9c2f-5d32-a1a1-ac39767edaa8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152983 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153196 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153196 #edb/ref 1061 3709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153196 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1aec00f2-ca4a-5296-be84-e7e83d0f585d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153196 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153199 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153199 #edb/ref 1061 3712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153199 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4dc61433-6c56-58c8-9a95-4614aa5f1bf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153199 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153200 #edb/ref 1061 3715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153200 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e8203c67-93c8-55d2-b5fd-7a7003ecaf79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153200 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153338 #edb/ref 1064 "How does a proportional relationship look when we plot it in the $(x,y)$ plane?\n\nTo answer this, let's consider the following proportional relationship:\n\n$$\ny = 2x\n$$\n\nTo plot a graph, we first need a table of values. \n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |\n| $1$ |  |\n| $2$ |  |\n| $3$ |  |\n\nFilling in the $y$-values using the equation $y = 2x,$ we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $3$ | $6$ |\n\nSo, we have the following $(x,y)$ coordinates:\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\quad (1,2),  \\quad (2,4),  \\quad (3,6)\n$$\n\nIf we plot these points in the coordinate plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/a8/a8cd951c66c67b8f28b791011c7553675b3112029d9ebe4b19769127b5e75bd5.png)\n\nThis leads us to the following important fact:\n\n*The graph of any proportional relationship is a straight line that passes through the origin $(0,0)$.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153338 #edb/ref 1065 1507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153338 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "becbb429-3b12-59c4-8172-aef250c47df9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153338 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153339 #edb/ref 1064 "When there is a proportional relationship between $x$ and $y,$ we can express this using the **proportionality symbol** $\\propto,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny \\propto x\n$$\n\nNote that the following statements are all equivalent:\n\n- $y = kx$\n\n- $y$ **varies directly** with $x$\n\n- $y$ is **directly proportional** to $x$\n\n- $y \\propto x$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153339 #edb/ref 1065 1508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153339 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "503f0382-0b82-583d-898a-bc1dd1aa02d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153339 #edb/ref 1067 "Alternative Notations and Terminology" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203942 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203942 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203942 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203942 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203942 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203942 #edb/ref 1039 "Quadratics With Positive Coefficients (After Initial Factoring)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203942 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186203744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203942 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "caf03377-f06d-5831-80b8-dcbf4978a8db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203943 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203943 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203943 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203943 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203943 #edb/ref 1039 "Quadratics With Negative Constants (After Initial Factoring)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203943 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186203745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203943 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b49cf980-1078-593b-bf56-c9a132c41273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203944 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203944 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203944 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203944 #edb/ref 1039 "Quadratics With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants (After Initial Factoring)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203944 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186203746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203944 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0e1a8957-9964-5eba-b5f4-b0e1dd525838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1045 "Further Factoring Trinomials With Leading Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186204741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "534c3fc9-4831-5fca-ad59-09b388796c6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204741 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204741 #edb/ref 1061 178290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204741 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "97ca27d9-5627-500f-b4ce-94d3d62acc77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204741 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204742 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204742 #edb/ref 1061 178291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204742 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "53a8db76-c802-563a-80d3-ee07f4085259" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204742 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204743 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204743 #edb/ref 1061 178295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204743 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "408a8b32-052b-5f8b-9893-3e4c9e92fd6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204743 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204744 #edb/ref 1061 178299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204744 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "73b63fbf-6b48-571e-9ab0-6b574e48cf7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204744 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205291 #edb/ref 1064 "When factoring a trinomial, we should always begin by factoring out any constant factors. Then, we factor whatever quadratic remains using the techniques we've studied. \n\nFor example, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n6y^2+21y+9\n$$\n\nThe first thing to notice is that all the terms have a common factor of $3.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n3(2y^2 + 7y + 3)\n$$\n\nSo, our goal now is to factor the trinomial in parentheses:\n\n$$\n2y^2 + 7y + 3\n$$\n\nTo do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times 3 = 6$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nAlso, since all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the *positive* factor pairs of $6.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(1,6),\\quad (2,3).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,6)$ add to $7{:}$ \n\n$$\n1+6 =7  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $y+6y,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}+7y+3= \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+y+6y+3 & = \\\\ (2{y}^{2}+y)+(6y+3) & = \\\\ y(2y+1)+3(2y+1) & = \\\\ (y+3)(2y+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{2}+21y+9 & = \\\\ 3(2{y}^{2}+7y+3) & = \\\\ 3(y+3)(2y+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nWhenever the coefficient of the quadratic term is negative, we should always factor this out first. That way, we're left with the task of factoring a trinomial containing a positive quadratic coefficient. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205291 #edb/ref 1065 9456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205291 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "35b6c679-c9c6-5b44-8de5-21106bc70674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205291 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233141 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111362" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233141 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233141 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that  $(3{s}^{2}+2s+4)({s}^{2}+3s+2)=3{s}^{4}+a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+cs+8,$ calculate $a+b+c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233141 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e08294c6-a979-573d-8467-4eac7fae6a3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233141 #edb/ref 1074 "We distribute all three terms of the trinomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{s}^{2}+2s+4)({s}^{2}+3s+2) & = \\\\ 3{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+3s+2)+(2s)({s}^{2}+3s+2)+(4)({s}^{2}+3s+2) & = \\\\ 3{s}^{4}+9{s}^{3}+6{s}^{2}+2{s}^{3}+6{s}^{2}+4s+4{s}^{2}+12s+8 & = \\\\ 3{s}^{4}+11{s}^{3}+16{s}^{2}+16s+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=11,$ $b=16,$ and $c=16.$ So, \n\n$$\na+b+c=11+16+16=43.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351989 #edb/ref 1080 "800" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351989 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351989 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2db54839-6a5b-5b75-9ac0-2fe3487ce8c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352191 #edb/ref 1080 "100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352191 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352191 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "43e30190-4e81-5fda-b3a5-079b052411c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352192 #edb/ref 1080 "40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352192 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352192 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "43f1421d-10d0-5af6-8262-60e55e5615e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352294 #edb/ref 1080 "200" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352294 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352294 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "50c4006c-d6f4-5cb3-977c-4236359a9cc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352666 #edb/ref 1080 "400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352666 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186